ng literal `password=supersecret123`-style strings, which no pattern can distinguish from real secrets) that held the ledger push back; as of 2026-07-26 `cledger scan` on this repo reports **no findings** — the allowlist has absorbed them, so the ledger push is no longer blocked. The open question this leaves is the roadmap's \"audit the allowlist for generalizable false-positive patterns\" item: allowlisting is per-repo, so every downstream consumer whose codebase talks *about* secrets hits the same `keyword-assignment` false positives independently.","oldString":"**Dogfooding note:** this repo captures its own sessions. The ledger still has ~64 benign scan findings (fixtures containing literal `password=supersecret123`-style strings, which no pattern can distinguish from real secrets), so the ledger push is held back by default while code pushes fine. Sharing this session's ledger requires resolving those with `cledger allow`.","originalFile":"---\nname: project-cledger-redaction-state\ndescription: \"conversation-ledger secret-redaction stack — what shipped (0.6.1/0.7.0), decisions made, and what's deferred\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: project\n  originSessionId: 48648da3-5c2c-4827-96d5-7a6ed13224df\n  modified: 2026-07-21T01:19:25.689Z\n---\n\nState of conversation-ledger's redaction work as of 2026-07-20.\n\n**Shipped:**\n- 0.6.1 — scan/sync finding reports fully mask the matched span (`<redacted>` + ~20 chars context + fingerprint, zero secret characters). Closes the sync-report channel of the capture/scan feedback loop.\n- 0.7.0 — opt-in `redact.knownSecrets` (default off). `cledger redact --pattern` remembers the exact scrubbed plaintext in `.git/conversation-ledger/known-secrets.json` (local, git-invisible, beside the allowlist); capture-time redaction then exact-matches those values out of every future draft under a `known-secret` rule id. Only `--pattern` feeds it (`--all` blanks whole content); sub-8-char values dropped. Enabled globally on this machine via `~/.config/cledger/config.json`.\n- 0.7.1 — `keyword-assignment` scan rule tightened (value charset excludes code punctuation, killing the type-annotation / template-interpolation false positives; this repo went 93 -> 64 findings); known-secrets store written `0600` with perm repair on each write; tests now redirect HOME to a throwaway dir so the developer's global config can't change test outcomes.\n\n**Decisions (so the next agent doesn't re-litigate):**\n- The roadmap's original \"make capture-tier a strict superset of scan-tier\" framing was considered and **rejected** — promoting the deliberately-noisy scan rules to run at capture would silently rewrite innocent text, breaking the near-zero-false-positive invariant. The value-based learning above is the intended narrow form.\n- Store holds plaintext by necessity (fingerprints are one-way, can't drive exact matching); acceptable because `.git/` is as local-and-unshared as the transcripts, per the transport trust boundary.\n\n**Deferred, roughly by urgency:**\n1. Encrypt known-secrets at rest (OS keychain; decrypt must be non-interactive since capture runs silently every turn). `0600` already shipped.\n2. Post-push purge, re-anchor, path-based capture exclusion, auto-renormalize, harness-artifact capture, sub-turn citation anchors.\n\n**Dogfooding note:** this repo captures its own sessions. The ledger still has ~64 benign scan findings (fixtures containing literal `password=supersecret123`-style strings, which no pattern can distinguish from real secrets), so the ledger push is held back by default while code pushes fine. Sharing this session's ledger requires resolving those with `cledger allow`.\n\nRelated: [[project-ledger-ecosystem]]\n","replaceAll":false,"structuredPatch":[{"lines":["   node_type: memory","   type: project","   originSessionId: 48648da3-5c2c-4827-96d5-7a6ed13224df","-  modified: 2026-07-21T01:19:25.689Z","+  modified: 2026-07-27T06:49:36.545Z"," ---"," "," State of conversation-ledger's redaction work as of 2026-07-20."],"newLines":7,"newStart":5,"oldLines":7,"oldStart":5},{"lines":[" 1. Encrypt known-secrets at rest (OS keychain; decrypt must be non-interactive since capture runs silently every turn). `0600` already shipped."," 2. Post-push purge, re-anchor, path-based capture exclusion, auto-renormalize, harness-artifact capture, sub-turn citation anchors."," ","-**Dogfooding note:** this repo captures its own sessions. The ledger still has ~64 benign scan findings (fixtures containing literal `password=supersecret123`-style strings, which no pattern can distinguish from real secrets), so the ledger push is held back by default while code pushes fine. Sharing this session's ledger requires resolving those with `cledger allow`.","+**Dogfooding note:** this repo captures its own sessions. It once had ~64 benign scan findings (fixtures containing literal `password=supersecret123`-style strings, which no pattern can distinguish from real secrets) that held the ledger push back; as of 2026-07-26 `cledger scan` on this repo reports **no findings** — the allowlist has absorbed them, so the ledger push is no longer blocked. The open question this leaves is the roadmap's \"audit the allowlist for generalizable false-positive patterns\" item: allowlisting is per-repo, so every downstream consumer whose codebase talks *about* secrets hits the same `keyword-assignment` false positives independently."," "," Related: [[project-ledger-ecosystem]]"],"newLines":6,"newStart":23,"oldLines":6,"oldStart":23}],"userModified":false},"type":"user","userType":"external","uuid":"b7086819-fc39-4a22-a20c-3b3090743025","version":"2.1.220"},"format":"claude-code-jsonl/1"},"recorded_at":"2026-07-27T06:49:53.428Z","schema":"conversation-ledger/v1"}
